commit 22880a96dd0307a53aee2b064104bbcf74804f3f
Author: MATTHEW M PEET <68134670+mmpeet@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Thu Sep 10 06:25:22 2026 -0700

    Build int_semisep's packed outputs from triplets, not by index assignment
    
    Profiling a large composition put 67.5% of int_semisep in a single
    statement -- C_gam_alp_beta{gamma}(rows,cols) = Cnew -- repeated 11^ns3a
    times into preallocated all-zero sparse matrices, rising to 79.5% at
    three pass-through variables. Its cost per nonzero moved GREW with the
    problem, 67.9 -> 131.0 ns, which is the signature of rebuilding the
    sparse structure on every write.
    
    Now each block's nonzeros are stashed as triplets, shifted onto their
    (beta,alpha) offset, and one 'sparse' call per gamma assembles the result
    after the loop. That is CLAUDE.md's rule applied literally: build sparse
    matrices once from triplets, never index-assign in a loop.
    
    Nothing needs summing, and that is a property of the key table rather
    than luck: the eleven rows of 'cllA' are distinct (gamma,beta,alpha)
    triples, so for a fixed (beta,alpha) the gammas differ, and the map from
    gamma-tuple to gam_lin is a bijection -- each (gamma,beta,alpha) block is
    written at most once. A gamma never written concatenates to empty, which
    'sparse' turns into the all-zero matrix the preallocation used to supply.
    Only the 'packed' layout is touched; 'separate' already assigned whole
    cells.
    
    Verified BIT-IDENTICAL: 28 captured A'*A and A'*B compositions over 7
    variable-group shapes at two degrees, 0.000e+00 relative difference
    against HEAD; and int_semisep called directly, old against new on
    identical inputs, 0.0e+00 at ns3a 1 to 3.
    
    End-to-end through @sopvar/mtimes, 5 reps, mean +- std:
      in{m,n,a}    out{w,y,z,m,n} deg3 dims[2 2]  1.113+-0.050 -> 0.875+-0.036  1.27x
      in{m,n,a}    out{w,y,z,m,n} deg3 dims[4 4]  3.005+-0.042 -> 2.756+-0.031  1.09x
      in{m,n,p,a}  out{y,z,m,n,p} deg2 dims[2 2]  3.677+-0.018 -> 2.175+-0.036  1.69x
    Differences of 0.25-1.5 s against standard deviations of 0.02-0.05 s, so
    this one is outside the noise, unlike the two attempts before it.
    
    161-check battery 0 failures; canonical-order sweep 0.00e+00 on 26 shapes
    x 1120 orderings with its negative control detecting; LPI stability still
    straddles pi^2 = 9.8696 at 462 decision variables.
    
