commit f40509e7d98172fe86cf03aa4cbe00c4a08d9ddc
Author: MATTHEW M PEET <68134670+mmpeet@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Wed Sep 9 03:51:14 2026 -0700

    Align the right operand of an sopvar composition by variable name
    
    vars.out = [S2,S3] and vars.in = [S3,S1] are now canonical, so a legal
    composition can present its two intermediate lists in different orders:
    for A'*A the adjoint's vars.in is [S3,S2] while A's vars.out is [S2,S3].
    The order guard in mtimes refused exactly those, which took out A'*A,
    A'*B and every self-adjointness check whenever S2 and S3 were both
    nonempty.
    
    Legality is now a question about the variable SETS. Once that holds, B's
    output side is permuted onto A's input order: B.ZL, B.dom.out and the row
    index of each parameter, the row being (matrix row outer, ZL monomial
    inner). Rows only, so the canonical multiplier form -- a condition on ZR
    columns -- is untouched, and it is done on the local copy rather than
    through the constructor, which would canonicalize B straight back.
    
    monomial_gather is split out of canonical_var_order for the tensor
    transposition of the monomial index, now that two callers need it.
    
    The same change in @sdopvar/mtimes_AT, which carried the same guard. It
    is not reachable from anything in the toolbox today -- every reference to
    it is a comment -- and the branch the alignment newly exposes asks for a
    122 GB array at two integrated variables and degrees (2,3), recorded in
    its header. It is correct where it fits in memory.
    
    Verified: 146 checks over 7 variable-group shapes, 0 failures, adjoint
    identity and A'*B == (B'*A)' at machine precision; mtimes_AT agreed with
    @sopvar/mtimes on the evaluated operator to 4e-15 wherever both ran; the
    canonical-order sweep still gives 0.00e+00 on 26 shapes x 1120 orderings
    with its negative control detecting; the LPI stability test still
    straddles pi^2 = 9.8696 at 462 decision variables.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

